  <p class="lede first reveal">The question that stalls more physical-product stores than any other is not <em>how do I build it</em>. It's <em>what on earth do I put in it</em>. You don't sew. You don't throw pots. So the whole idea feels like it belongs to someone else.</p>
  <p class="reveal">It doesn't. Almost nobody running a small online store makes the thing they sell. A supplier prints the mug, stitches the sweatshirt. What the store owner brings is a decision: who this is for, and why she'd want it. That decision is the product.</p>
  <p class="reveal">This piece is about making that decision well: with a person, a moment in her year, and four filters that stop you listing things nobody will buy.</p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="person">Stop looking for a product; look for a person and a moment</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Pick one woman you understand, then list the moments that come round in her year. The products fall out of the moments. Starting from a product and hunting for a buyer is backwards.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Product-first thinking sounds like this: <em>mugs are popular, what could I put on a mug?</em> It produces a hundred ideas and no reason for anyone to choose yours. Person-first sounds like this: <em>a nurse on nights, three weeks into a new rotation. What does she reach for, give, or wish existed?</em> It produces six ideas, each with a buyer attached.</p>
  <p class="reveal">The person has to be someone you actually understand. Not a demographic; a woman whose week you could describe hour by hour. The nurse on nights. The mum homeschooling at the kitchen table since September. The first-time dog owner who is four days in and hasn't slept. If you've been her, or lived next to her, you know things a stranger would have to guess.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Then the moments. Every person has a calendar of events that come round whether she's ready or not. For the nurse: a colleague's leaving do, the student she mentored qualifying, a Christmas shift she didn't want. For the homeschool mum: the first day of term at home, the end-of-year "we did it". For the dog owner: the day the puppy comes home, the first Christmas with a dog in the photo.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Write the person at the top of a page and ten moments underneath. Beside each, write what a physical object would do there: mark it, celebrate it, make it easier, or say something she's thinking but wouldn't say out loud. That page is the product list you were told you'd never be able to make.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>The build order in this house never changes: offer first, store second, automation third, audience last. For a physical store that reads product first, store second, fulfilment app third, buyers last. This chapter is "product first", and it happens on paper before you open an app.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/choosing-a-niche">How to Choose Your Niche in a Weekend →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="filters">The four filters</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">A candidate product passes if she'd buy it without a video, it's gift-able or tied to an occasion, a supplier already makes it well, and you can describe it in one sentence. Fail one and it goes back on the list.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Your page of moments will have too many ideas on it. Good. Run each one through four questions, in order, and be strict.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>She'd buy it without a video.</strong> You're building a faceless store around a day job. You won't be filming demonstrations. So the product has to make sense from a photo and a line of text. A sweatshirt that says something a night-shift nurse feels needs no explanation. A gadget that needs a tutorial does.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>It's gift-able or occasion-tied.</strong> The strongest small-store products are bought <em>for</em> someone, or <em>because</em> of something. A gift has a deadline and a budget attached; the buyer isn't asking "do I need this" but "will she like this". An occasion means the search comes back every year on the same date. Impulse buys for oneself, with no occasion behind them, are the hardest sell a beginner can pick.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A supplier already makes it well.</strong> Open Printful, Printify or Gelato, or one of the US-warehoused dropshipping apps, and check that the blank exists, that its reviews are decent, and that it ships to where your person lives in reasonable time. If you'd have to compromise on the item to make it work, it's the wrong item. The supplier's catalogue is the boundary of what you can sell, and it's a large boundary.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>You can describe it in one sentence.</strong> <em>A heavyweight mug for nurses on nights, with a line about the 3am slump.</em> If you need a paragraph, the listing title will be a mess and search won't find it.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The four filters</span><p>No video needed · Gift-able or occasion-tied · A supplier already makes it well · One-sentence description. The first filter kills the most, so start there.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/boring-first-passive-stream">Your First Passive Stream Should Be Boring →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="ideas">Print on demand product ideas that actually move</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Four categories carry most first stores: apparel for an identity, drinkware tied to a role, wall prints for a room, and notebooks or planners built around a routine. Each one maps to a person and a moment.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Print on demand is the beginner door: nothing is made until it's ordered, so there's no stock to buy and no box of unsold sweatshirts in the spare room. Within that door, these are the shelves that move.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Apparel for an identity.</strong> A sweatshirt sells when the words on it say something the wearer believes about herself and wants a specific group to see. One line of good type on a quality blank beats a cluttered graphic, and it's cheaper to sample.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Mugs and tumblers tied to a role.</strong> The classic gift-with-a-deadline. Somebody's colleague is leaving, and a mug that names the role and gets the tone right is the safe, kind present.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Wall prints for a room.</strong> A print is bought when a room changes: a nursery, a home-school corner, a home office that's finally hers. Sets of three sell better than singles because they solve the whole wall.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Notebooks and planners for a routine.</strong> A cover plus a sensible interior, made for one routine: a shift log, a homeschool week, a puppy's first-year record. The interior is where the specificity lives, and specificity is what makes a stranger feel it was made for her.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Order a sample of anything you plan to sell, before you list it. Printify is a marketplace of print providers, so quality varies by who prints it. One sample costs a little now; a run of bad reviews costs the store.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/print-on-demand-vs-dropshipping">Print on Demand vs Dropshipping: Which to Start With →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="avoid">What to avoid</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Skip trend products, anything with a licensed character, team or band, anything fragile or heavy, anything with sizes you can't sample, and anything a big retailer already sells cheaper. Each one costs more than it earns.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Trend products.</strong> The item everyone in your feed suddenly has. By the time you've sampled and listed it, a hundred other stores have too, and the search is fading. A trend can be a second product once something steady is underneath it. It's a terrible first one.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Anything with a licensed character, team or band.</strong> A cartoon character on a tee, a sports team logo on a mug, a band name in their font. The print-on-demand apps remove the design; repeat offences close the account. Recolouring doesn't make it legal. Build around your person's role instead, which nobody owns.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Fragile or heavy items.</strong> Glassware, framed art with real glass, anything whose box costs more than the product. Breakage is your problem, and heavy postage is why your price looks wrong.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Sizes you can't sample.</strong> Print on demand is made to order, so there are no returns for a wrong size or a change of mind; you pay for reprints unless it's a defect. If you can't check the sizes, you don't know what you're selling. Start with one-size items or a size chart you've tested yourself.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Anything a big retailer sells cheaper.</strong> A plain black tee. A generic "world's best mum" mug. If a supermarket has it for less, your only argument is the design, and it had better be very specific to your person.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The avoid list</span><p>Trend items · Licensed characters, teams, bands · Fragile or heavy · Sizes you haven't sampled · Beaten on price by a big retailer.</p></div>
  <p class="reveal">One more, for the dropshipping side of the door. If you sell ready-made goods, use only suppliers with stock warehoused in your customer's country. The US de minimis duty exemption ended in 2025, so China-direct parcels now arrive with duties, brokerage fees and delays that land on your customer, and on your reviews.</p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="demand">Checking demand without a following</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Search the plain phrase on Etsy, Amazon and Google to see if it already exists and sells. Then read the reviews of the products that do, looking for the complaint you can fix. You don't need an audience to do either.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Search the phrase.</strong> Type the one-sentence description, in your person's words, into Etsy, then Amazon, then Google. Do listings exist, with reviews? Reviews mean orders. Dozens of listings with hundreds of reviews means demand is proven and your job is to be specific. Nothing at all is suspicious; an empty category is usually empty for a reason.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Read the two- and three-star reviews.</strong> Five stars tell you the product works. Two and three stars tell you what's wrong with it, in the buyer's own words: the print faded, the mug was smaller than the photo, the planner had no space for night shifts. Whatever complaint repeats is the gap. Your version fixes that one thing and says so in the listing.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Ask where those people already are.</strong> Your person has a place she talks: a forum, a group, a thread. Read for an hour before you post. Then, if the group allows it, ask plainly: "If you were buying a gift for a colleague finishing her first year, what would you actually want?" You aren't selling. You're checking the moment is real, and collecting her words for it.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Write down the exact phrases from the reviews and the group. Those are your listing titles and tags. The buyer has told you how she searches; don't improve on it.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/shopify-store-guide">How to Start a Shopify Store →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">06</span><div class="num">Chapter Six</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="three">Pick three, launch one</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Take three candidates that passed every filter, put one live, and keep two ready. The first product exists to teach you the whole cycle, not to be the one that makes the store.</p>
  <p class="reveal">You now have a page of moments, a shortlist that survived four filters, and evidence from other people's reviews. Pick the three strongest. Then pick one, and only that one goes live.</p>
  <p class="reveal">This isn't caution. A first product is a learning product. It teaches you how long a sample takes and what it looks like in real light, how a listing photo behaves on a phone, where fulfilment breaks. You can't learn that from three products faster than from one.</p>
  <p class="reveal">The other two are queued, not wasted. Once the first has been ordered, shipped and reviewed a few times, you'll know something about your person you didn't before, and one of the waiting products will look obviously right. Add it. Then the third. Three products chosen this way outsell forty listed in a hurry.</p>
  <p class="reveal">With the product decided, the store comes second. <a href="https://shopify.pxf.io/eK3xYz" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Shopify's $1-a-month start</a> (affiliate link) gives you a three-day free trial, then three months at $1 a month on monthly billing: enough time to put one product live, sample it and take a first order. Shopify's prices are theirs and subject to change.</p>
  <p class="reveal">If you'd rather work the decision through a worksheet, <a href="/resources/shopify-starter-kit"><strong>The Shopify Starter Kit</strong></a> is free and covers this stage. The full build, lesson by lesson, is <strong>The Shopify Edit</strong> inside the <a href="/membership">Membership Standard ($9/month or $99/year, 7-day free trial)</a>.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The rule</span><p>Three candidates on paper. One live. Two queued until the first has been ordered, shipped and reviewed. That's the whole launch plan.</p></div>
